> IMO we need a clear and well-defined OFFICIAL definition for detri, not=
=20
> several contradicting ad-hoc definitions.=20
>
>
Those different answers gave you some different examples of convention,=20
which are not suggestion for official definition.
The official definition of {detri} does not restrict the form of x1 to one=
=20
convention: x1 of {detri} is any sumti that can be a symbol for a time=20
point; the applicable symbol is defined with x4.
The form of x1 depends on context, and you can specify the form with x4 or=
=20
any additional items like {fi'o}, {noi}, {ti'o}* and so on if necessary.

(*Use of {ti'o} for that purpose is not officially suggested but I think it=
=20
is enough applicable for specifying mapping of numbers to time points.)=20

According to your answers so far, I can't even safely make sense out of=20
> something like =E2=80=9Cli pa pi'e re pi'e ci detri=E2=80=9D, because you=
 don't seem=20
> to even agree on the ordering. :-(=20
>


The official definition for {detri} should not specify the ordering because=
=20
{detri} has x4 by nature to specify the calendar that defines the system of=
=20
mapping of symbols to time points. If you think the current definition is=
=20
confusing, we need to add explanation of usage of x4, not restriction to=20
the form of x1.



Le mardi 3 mai 2016 16:18:52 UTC, la gleki a =C3=A9crit :
>
>
>
> Well, Lojban for Beginners and "lerfu detri" don't contradict each other.=
=20
> Otherwise, yes.
>
>
No, as explained above, those are different conventions and not definitions=
=20
of x1 of {detri}. They all can be used in different contexts. Because the=
=20
context should be different between them, the universe of discourse is=20
different between them, and therefore they cannot contradict each other.

=20

> There is also one more solution: not to use {detri} at all. You may use=
=20
> {jednpa} etc., {de'i'u}...
>
>
 As explained above, you can stay with {detri} by declaring x4 of {detri}.=
=20
 {jednpa} series and {de'i'u} series are all defined with {detri} and they=
=20
can be expanded to {detri} form of the same meaning.


mi'e la guskant

